9	13	\N	22	Yes, I would! 3 full days in Jaipur feels perfect not too rushed, but still enough to cover the must-sees.\n\nJaipur is super solo-travel friendly because most attractions are close to each other, and it’s easy to move around with a driver or Uber. Plus, the city has so many iconic spots that you won’t get bored even if you stay in one place.\n\nIf anything, I’d rather stay 3 days in Jaipur than stress over moving cities constantly 😅\nHow about you would you do Jaipur only too, or would you add a quick day trip like Pushkar/Agra?	f	0	active	[]	\N	2026-02-01 03:29:21.784+00	2026-02-01 03:29:21.784+00	f	0	f
10	15	\N	19	What do you think people are really searching for when they wake up at 3AM just to see Bromo sunrise?	f	0	active	[]	\N	2026-02-04 05:30:39.259+00	2026-02-04 05:30:39.259+00	f	0	f
11	18	\N	28	My most unforgettable trekking moment wasn’t at the summit.\n\nAs a woman, it was the moment I wanted to stop but didn’t. My legs were tired, my breath felt heavy, and then the view suddenly opened up. I remember thinking, “Oh… I can actually do this.”\n\nThat quiet confidence stayed with me long after the hike ended 🥾✨	f	0	active	[]	\N	2026-02-06 02:02:50.204+00	2026-02-06 02:02:50.204+00	f	0	f
12	15	10	28	@Firman Maulana I think people aren’t really chasing the sunrise itself.\n\nThey’re searching for a quiet moment when the world is still dark, their thoughts slow down, and they can feel small but grounded at the same time.\nBromo at 3AM feels like a pause button we don’t get in everyday life.	f	0	active	[]	\N	2026-02-06 02:07:29.342+00	2026-02-06 02:07:29.343+00	f	0	f
13	15	12	19	@Yina meiling I don’t think it’s just about the sunrise either.\n\nMaybe people wake up at 3AM for Bromo because they’re looking for something that feels bigger than their routine. When you stand there in the cold, waiting in the dark, there’s a strange honesty to it. No distractions. No noise. Just you and the mountain.\n\nI feel like it’s about proving to yourself that some things are still worth the effort, even losing sleep. That beauty sometimes asks for sacrifice.\n\nAnd when the sun finally rises, it’s not just light hitting the volcano. It’s relief. It’s perspective. It’s that quiet reminder that the world keeps moving and so do we.\n\nMaybe what people are really searching for at 3AM… is clarity.	f	0	active	[]	\N	2026-02-12 03:11:59.672+00	2026-02-12 03:11:59.672+00	f	0	f
14	13	9	19	@Eduardo Saverin I agree, 3 full days in Jaipur sounds like the sweet spot. Not rushed, but still productive enough to really enjoy the city.\n\nPersonally, I think I’d focus on Jaipur only. Sometimes staying in one place a little longer lets you feel the rhythm of the city, not just checking off landmarks, but actually enjoying slow mornings, local cafés, and sunset views without watching the clock.\n\nThat said, Pushkar feels tempting for a quick change of atmosphere. Agra too, of course, but I feel like the Taj Mahal deserves its own trip instead of being squeezed into a tight schedule.\n\nIf you had to choose just one day trip "Pushkar or Agra"  which one would you prioritize and why?	f	0	active	[]	\N	2026-02-12 03:13:49.491+00	2026-02-12 03:13:49.491+00	f	0	f
