18	28	\N	19	Is the Essential Historic Tour in Kalgoorlie truly worth it, or is exploring the town independently a better experience?	f	0	active	[]	\N	2026-02-16 05:22:30.376+00	2026-02-16 05:22:30.376+00	f	0	f
19	28	18	18	@Firman Maulana If you enjoy getting deep historical context and behind-the-scenes stories, the Essential Historic Tour is definitely worth it. Kalgoorlie might look like a small outback town, but its gold rush history is rich and layered. A guided tour usually gives you insights about the Super Pit, early prospectors, and the social history along Hannan Street that you might miss if you just read information boards on your own.\n\nHowever, if you prefer a slower, more flexible experience, walking around at your own pace, visiting museums, stopping by cafés, and taking photos exploring independently can be just as rewarding. The town is relatively easy to navigate on your own.\n\nIdeally, you could join the tour first to understand the bigger picture, then explore independently to make the experience feel more personal. But in the end, it really depends on your travel style.	f	0	active	[]	\N	2026-02-17 03:27:46.699+00	2026-02-17 03:27:46.699+00	f	0	f
20	24	16	18	@Eduardo Saverin That’s such an interesting question.\n\nFor me, Surabaya feels more raw and bold, the flavors are stronger, the energy is louder, and the streets feel unapologetically alive. It has that gritty, real-city character that makes the experience feel intense and memorable.\n\nMalang, on the other hand, feels softer and more nostalgic. The cooler weather, colonial architecture, and slower pace create a kind of comforting authenticity, less overwhelming, but deeply charming.\n\nSo I’d say Surabaya feels more “authentic” in a bold, unfiltered way, while Malang feels authentic in a warm, intimate way. It really depends on what kind of connection you’re looking for.	f	0	active	[]	\N	2026-02-17 03:29:06.836+00	2026-02-17 03:29:06.836+00	f	0	f
