235	3	22	Exploring London on a Budget: Local Experiences Beyond the Tourist Spots	exploring-london-on-a-budget-local-experiences-beyond-the-tourist-spots	London can be expensive, but there are many ways to enjoy the city without spending too much. I loved exploring local neighborhoods, visiting free museums, walking through parks, and trying affordable street food.\n\nBudget travel made me discover a more authentic side of London beyond the famous landmarks.\n\nWhat are your favorite budget-friendly experiences, hidden gems, or cheap local food spots in London? Share your tips with fellow travelers!	f	f	f	118	4	3	2026-09-11 13:20:41.773+00	22	active	[]	56	Exploring London on a Budget: Local Experiences Beyond th...	London can be expensive, but there are many ways to enjoy the city without spending too much. I loved exploring local neighborhoods, visiting free museums, w...	london, uk travel, budget travel, local experience, backpacker	\N	2026-09-11 13:08:59.556+00	2026-10-14 18:18:20.628+00	0
25	5	19	Chasing Wind and Waves: The Reality of Photographing Surfing & Kitesurfing in Yangyang, Korea	chasing-wind-and-waves-the-reality-of-photographing-surfing-and-kitesurfing-in-yangyang-korea	![Image](/images/stories/content/content-1770865772073-882545386.jpg)\n\n## Why Yangyang?\n\nIf you follow Korea’s surf scene, you know Yangyang on the east coast is the place.\n\nStrong wind.\nClean horizon.\nRaw energy.\n\nAs a male photographer, I came here chasing action shots, boards cutting waves, kites slicing through the sky. But what I found was more complex than adrenaline.\n\n\n## The Beautiful Part\n\n### Light, Motion, and Pure Freedom\n\nThe east coast light hits differently.\n\nEarly mornings give you soft golden reflections on the water. Late afternoons bring dramatic shadows under flying kites. Every frame feels alive.\n\nSurfing and kitesurfing are unpredictable and that’s the beauty of it.\nYou don’t control the moment. You wait for it.\n\nWhen a surfer lands perfectly under a rising wave, or when a kite catches the wind at just the right angle, that split second feels worth everything.\n\n\n## The Hard Part\n\n### Wind, Sand, and Patience\n\nNo one talks about this enough.\n\n> Sand inside your camera body.\n\n> Saltwater mist everywhere.\n\n> Wind so strong it shakes your lens.\n\n> Hours of waiting for one perfect frame.\n\nYour hands get cold.\nYour shoulders ache from holding long lenses.\nSometimes the waves don’t cooperate.\n\nAnd yet, you stay.\n\nBecause action photography is a test of endurance as much as skill.\n\n\n## What Surf Photography Taught Me\n\nYangyang taught me patience.\n\nIt taught me to respect nature instead of fighting it.\nTo understand wind patterns.\nTo read waves like a language.\n\nAs a man behind the camera, I realized something, strength here is not about dominance. It’s about adaptation.\n\nYou adjust.\nYou observe.\nYou wait.\n\n\n## Let’s Discuss\n\n👉 For those who have photographed surfing or kitesurfing? \n\n👉 Do you prefer shooting from the shore or in the water?\n\n👉 How do you protect your gear from sand and salt?\n\n👉 Do you focus more on action shots or lifestyle moments around the beach?\n\nIs Yangyang your favorite surf spot in Korea, or do you recommend others?\nI would love to hear your experiences.\n\nBecause chasing wind and waves is never just about the photo, it’s about the process.	f	f	f	122	0	0	\N	\N	active	[]	8	Chasing Wind and Waves: The Reality of Photographing Surf...	![Image](/images/stories/content/content-1770865772073-882545386.jpg)\n\n## Why Yangyang?\n\nIf you follow Korea’s surf scene, you know Yangyang on the east coas...	YangyangKorea, EastCoastKorea, SurfingKorea, KitesurfingKorea, ActionPhotography, BeachCulture, OutdoorAdventure	\N	2026-02-12 03:09:38.948+00	2026-10-15 10:41:54.063+00	0
162	7	18	Is Surabaya Indonesia's Ultimate Food Paradise? Let's Discuss!	is-surabaya-indonesias-ultimate-food-paradise-lets-discuss	Many people don't think of Surabaya as a major tourist destination, but I believe it's a city every food lover should visit at least once.\n\nSurabaya is famous for its incredible culinary scene. From authentic street food to legendary local restaurants, the city offers a wide variety of delicious dishes at affordable prices. Whether you're looking for traditional East Javanese flavors or hidden local favorites, there's always something worth trying.\n\nIf you've been to Surabaya, what food impressed you the most? Which restaurants, street stalls, or local specialties would you recommend to first-time visitors?\n\nI'd love to hear your favorite food spots, must-try dishes, and local tips. Let's help fellow travelers discover why Surabaya deserves a place on every foodie's travel list! 🍜🇮🇩	f	f	f	145	10	8	2026-07-03 11:40:31.681+00	18	active	[]	\N	Is Surabaya Indonesia's Ultimate Food Paradise? Let's Dis...	Many people don't think of Surabaya as a major tourist destination, but I believe it's a city every food lover should visit at least once.\n\nSurabaya is famou...	surabaya, indonesia, food, streetfood, culinary, localfood, traveltips, southeastasia, foodie, traveldiscussion	\N	2026-07-03 07:47:05.862+00	2026-10-14 12:04:47.672+00	0
196	7	212	Surga Maratua, Kami Mencintai Dasar Laut Biru Kehijauan	surga-maratua-kami-mencintai-dasar-laut-biru-kehijauan	Salah satu pecahan surga ada di Kalimantan Timur, tepatnya di Kepulauan Derawan, dimana pasir putih memanjang dengan udara laut yang sangat jernih. Kemudian manusia membangun tempat nyaman untuk menikmati surga ini.\nSaya menuju Pulau Maratua dengan perjalanan speed boat sekitar 3 jam dari Berau, Kalimantan Timur. Kami merapat di dermaga Maratua Paradise Resort, penginapan yang sebagian dibangun di atas air laut yang menutupi pasir putih pantai yang membentang luas, membentuk warna pirus yang indah saat matahari bersinar terang.\nBagian depan Maratua Paradise menghadap langsung dengan laut tiga warna, turqoise, biru kehijauan dan biru pekat karena kedalaman laut yang langsung berubah pada jarak sekitar 60 meter dari bagian depan penginapan. Semua bagian penginapan ini dibangun dengan kayu, dipadukan dengan laut.\nDi depan terdapat semacam teras untuk bersantai menikmati laut, dan yang saya suka adalah di tengah antara teras dan restoran terdapat semacam kolam kecil yang menyambung ke laut, dimana banyak ikan berseliweran yang dapat kita lihat dari tempat duduk.\nBagian terbaik dari lokasi ini adalah Water Villa, beberapa unit penginapan yang dibangun di atas air laut warna turqoise. Meski dibangun di atas laut, pasokan air tawar dialirkan melalui pipa tersendiri. Namun sayang, pembuangan dari saluran bathtub dan shower masih langsung ke bawah, ke laut, membuat saya enggan menggunakan banyak sampo dan sabun.\nDi luar saluran air yang masih belum dibuat terpisah, Maratua Paradise adalah lokasi yang sempurna untuk menikmati laut di Kepulauan Derawan. Teras unit water villa dimana saya menginap langsung menghadap laut biru, meski berada di atas laut pun unit kamar dibuat sangat nyaman.\nUntuk menikmati water villa anda harus menyiapkan sekitar 660 ribu rupiah per malam per orang, disini perhitungan tarif per orang karena harga sekian sudah termasuk makan tiga kali sehari karena tentunya tidak ada warung makan yang jaraknya dekat dari resort ini. Jika mau sedikit menghemat ada bagian villa pantai yang terletak di pesisir pantai, dengan harga 550 ribu rupiah per malam per orang. Namun menurut saya , sensasi menginap di water villa jauh berbeda dengan di beach villa, terserah Anda.\nIni adalah lokasi di mana Anda ingin waktu berhenti, dan setiap matahari bersinar terik adalah waktu terbaik di mana air laut berubah warna menjadi sempurna, dan Anda tidak dapat menahan keinginan untuk berenang seperti Dory. Berenang terus, berenang terus, berenang terus berenang.\n\n**CATATAN :**\n\n● Harga menginap di Maratua Paradise Resort: Rp. 660.000,- per orang per malam untuk Water Villa, dan Rp. 550.000,- per orang per malam untuk Beach Villa. Harga sudah termasuk makan tiga kali sehari. Cek harga terlebih dahulu karena dapat berubah di saat high season.\n\n● Pulau Maratua terletak di Kepulauan Derawan, Kalimantan Timur. Ada dua cara akses ke Derawan, pertama ke Balikpapan terlebih dahulu, kemudian mengambil penerbangan lanjutan ke Berau atau ke Tarakan. Kemudian dari Tarakan atau Berau ke Kepulauan Derawan dengan menggunakan speed boat. Tarif speed boat dari pelabuhan Tanjung Batu, Berau ke Pulau Maratua Rp. 1.500.000,- (sekali jalan, kapasitas 10 hingga 13 orang). Lebih baik sewa untuk PP (diantar dan dijemput kembali, dengan harga Rp. 3.000.000,-)\n\n● Dari bandara Tanjung Redep Berau ke Pelabuhan Tanjung Batu, jika beramai ramai bisa sewa mobil, tarif PP (diantar dan dijemput saat kembali) sekitar Rp. 800.000,- untuk mobil berkapasitas 6 orang.\n\n● Waktu terbaik mengunjungi Maratua antara lain di bulan Maret hingga Mei, setelah bulan Agustus, awal bulan Oktober. Sementara di Akhir Oktober biasanya sudah memasuki masa sering turun hujan.\n\n● Alternatif penginapan murah di Pulau Maratua: Ananda Hotel dengan rate: Rp. 200.000,- per kamar (tanpa AC) dan Rp. 300.000,- per kamar AC. Ananda Hotel terletak sekitar 15 menit perjalanan naik motor dari Maratua Paradise Resort. Tapi pemandangan di Ananda sangat berbeda dengan di Surga Maratua. Saya sarankan setidaknya menginap satu hari saja di Maratua Paradise Resort.	f	f	f	130	2	3	2026-08-09 11:31:32.198+00	201	active	[]	6	Surga Maratua, Kami Mencintai Dasar Laut Biru Kehijauan	Salah satu pecahan surga ada di Kalimantan Timur, tepatnya di Kepulauan Derawan, dimana pasir putih memanjang dengan udara laut yang sangat jernih. Kemudian ...	indonesia, maratua, snorkeling, kalimantan timur	\N	2026-08-04 07:42:03.973+00	2026-10-15 09:55:29.595+00	0
2	7	12	NuArt Bandung: Bali-Style Art & Culture Vibes in the Middle of Bandung	nuart-bandung-bali-style-art-and-culture-vibes-in-the-middle-of-bandung	![Image](/images/stories/content/content-1768789811841-733219048.jpg)\nHi guys! 👋\n\nJust wanted to share something and open a casual discussion about **NuArt Sculpture Park Bandung**.\n\nIf you love Bali—especially Ubud’s calm art scene—and want a similar vibe without leaving Bandung, **NuArt really feels like a small “Bali-style art escape” in the city**.\n\nIt’s not loud, not crowded, and not just about taking photos. It’s more about feeling the space.\n\n### What Is NuArt Bandung All About?\n\n\nNuArt Sculpture Park is an art space created by **Nyoman Nuarta**, the famous Indonesian sculptor behind the Garuda Wisnu Kencana statue in Bali.\n\nThis place isn’t just a gallery. It’s a mix of:\n\nHuge and meaningful sculptures\n\nCool architecture with artistic details\n\nGreen open spaces\n\nA peaceful, reflective atmosphere\n\nWalking around NuArt feels slow and calming—kind of like strolling through an art space in Ubud.\n\n### Why People Say NuArt Feels Like “Bali in Bandung”\n\nHere’s why many visitors get Bali vibes from NuArt:\n\nThe artist is Balinese, so the philosophy behind the art feels deep\n\nLots of open space and nature, not closed rooms\n\nCalm, spiritual, and cultural mood\n\nPerfect for slow travel, healing, or just clearing your mind\n\nIt’s definitely not a “rush” destination. You come here to relax and think.\n\n## Other Art &amp; Culture Places in Bandung You Might Like\n\nIf NuArt is your kind of place, these spots might also be worth checking out:\n\n### 1. Selasar Sunaryo Art Space (Dago)\n\nContemporary art exhibitions\n\nCool weather and green surroundings\n\nChill place to enjoy art without pressure\n\nGreat if you like modern art with a peaceful vibe.\n\n### 2. Lawangwangi Creative Space (Dago Atas)\n\nArt gallery + café combo\n\nAmazing city view\n\nGood spot to enjoy art, coffee, and sunsets\n\nFeels like an Ubud-style creative space but with a Bandung twist.\n\n### 3. Saung Angklung Udjo\n\nStrong local Sundanese culture\n\nInteractive music performances\n\nFun and educational\n\nIf NuArt is more quiet and reflective, Saung Udjo is lively and cultural.\n\n### 4. Dago Tea House / West Java Cultural Park\n\nTraditional art performances\n\nGreen open area\n\nA place where local artists gather\n\nSimple, cultural, and full of local soul.\n\n## Let’s Talk! Forum Discussion Questions 💬\n\nCurious to hear your thoughts, so here are some questions:\n\n**Do you see NuArt more as an art gallery or a cultural tourism spot?**\n\n**Should Bandung have more places like NuArt? Where would be a good area for it?**\n\n**Compared to Ubud, what do you think Bandung does better in art &amp; culture?**\n\n**Which art or culture place in Bandung made you feel something emotionally?**\n\n**Do people in Bandung appreciate art spaces enough, or not yet?**\n\n## Final Thoughts\n\nNuArt shows that you don’t need to go to Bali to enjoy meaningful art and culture. Bandung already has the soul—it just needs more spaces like this.\n\nNow your turn 👇\n\n**Any other art or culture spots in Bandung that feel like hidden gems?**\n\nLet’s share and discuss! ✨	f	f	f	40	1	1	2026-01-19 03:53:26.444+00	6	active	[]	32	NuArt Bandung: Bali-Style Art & Culture Vibes in the Midd...	![Image](/images/stories/content/content-1768789811841-733219048.jpg)\nHi guys! 👋\n\nJust wanted to share something and open a casual discussion about **NuArt ...		\N	2026-01-19 02:33:33.734+00	2026-09-23 17:58:20.357+00	0
119	3	19	An Evening in Kampung Cina, Where Food and Old Streets Stay Alive	an-evening-in-kampung-cina-where-food-and-old-streets-stay-alive	![Image](/images/stories/content/content-1779085894386-301545920.jpg)\n\n## Kampung Cina After Sunset\n\nI went to Kampung Cina thinking I would only look for dinner.\n\nBut the moment I stepped into the old street, I realized this place was not just about food. It was the kind of neighborhood where history stayed between the buildings, where old shop houses stood quietly, and every corner seemed to hold the smell of something being cooked.\n\nThe street felt alive in a way that was hard to explain. Warm lights. People talking. Plates clinking. The kind of evening that made you slow down without planning to.\n\nHave you ever found a place where eating somehow became part of remembering?\n\n\n## More Than Just Food Stalls\n\nKampung Cina sits in the center of Padang, but once I entered its streets, the city outside felt distant.\n\nThe old architecture gave everything a different rhythm. Chinese-style buildings stood close together, some fading with age, some painted brightly, but all carrying stories that felt older than the traffic outside.\n\nFood stalls lined the road, simple and unpretentious. Nothing looked designed for tourists. That made it feel more honest.\n\nI walked slowly, not because the street was crowded, but because every few steps there was another smell that made me stop.\n\n### The Taste That Stayed Longer Than Expected\n\nI ordered food from a small stall where steam rose from metal pots and the owner barely looked up before smiling.\n\nThe meal was simple. Warm noodles, grilled snacks, sweet drinks served in old glasses.\n\nBut something about eating there made it feel more personal. Maybe it was the street around me. Maybe the old lanterns hanging above. Maybe the sound of motorcycles passing while people kept laughing at nearby tables.\n\nIt was ordinary in the best way.\n\nThe kind of food that becomes unforgettable not because it is expensive, but because the place gives it meaning.\n\n\n## A Street That Asks to Be Photographed\n\nI brought my camera, but after a while, I stopped taking photos of food.\n\nThe buildings caught my attention more. Narrow alleys, old windows, red details, faded walls touched by evening light.\n\nKampung Cina felt made for wandering without purpose. I kept finding small scenes that looked cinematic someone carrying groceries, a bicycle against an old door, smoke rising from a food cart under yellow lights.\n\nEven without trying, the street turned everything into a photo.\n\nAnd somehow, none of it felt staged.\n\n### The Part I Didn’t Expect\n\nWhat stayed with me was not only the meal.\n\nIt was how the street felt when night came. The mix of old stories and simple food. The way strangers sat close to each other without needing introductions. The way the lights reflected on old walls that had seen decades pass.\n\nKampung Cina reminded me that some places do not need famous landmarks.\n\nSometimes all they need is one warm plate of food, one old street, and enough time to walk without rushing.\n\n\n## Anyone Else Been to Kampung Cina?\n\nI went there for food, but I left feeling like I had walked through part of Padang’s memory.\n\nIf you’ve been to Kampung Cina, what did you try there? Any hidden food stalls, favorite dishes, or photo spots worth finding at night?	f	f	f	122	0	0	\N	\N	active	[]	17	An Evening in Kampung Cina, Where Food and Old Streets St...	![Image](/images/stories/content/content-1779085894386-301545920.jpg)\n\n## Kampung Cina After Sunset\n\nI went to Kampung Cina thinking I would only look for di...	kampungcina, culinarytravel, foodinpadang, streetfoodindonesia, chinatownpadang, localfoodexperience, traveldiscussion	\N	2026-05-18 06:32:17.972+00	2026-10-15 10:49:00.16+00	0
