37	1	19	BMA Dog Park Bangkok: Best Pet-Friendly Spot in Lumpini & Benjakitti?	bma-dog-park-bangkok-best-pet-friendly-spot-in-lumpini-and-benjakitti	![Image](/images/stories/content/content-1771906345337-800147006.jpg)\n\nAs a dog owner, finding a truly pet-friendly space while traveling is not always easy.\nBut in Bangkok, I found something refreshing: BMA Dog Park, located inside Lumpini Park and Benjakitti Park.\n\nBoth parks offer designated spaces where dogs can actually run free, socialize, and just be dogs — not just leashed companions on sidewalks.\n\n\n## Why I Like This Place\n\nThe atmosphere feels relaxed and welcoming.\nEarly mornings and late afternoons are the best time — cooler weather, friendly local dog owners, and a good mix of small and large breeds.\n\nThere’s a sense of community here. People don’t just bring their pets to exercise; they stay, talk, and connect. \n\n\n## Lumpini vs Benjakitti – Which One is Better?\n\n### Lumpini Park Dog Zone\n\n• More central location\n\n• Shaded areas with mature trees\n\n• Feels more classic and green\n\n### Benjakitti Park Dog Zone\n\n• More modern park layout\n\n• Cleaner open space\n\n• Great sunset atmosphere\n\n![Image](/images/stories/content/content-1771906734403-826176079.jpg)\n\nPersonally, I enjoy Benjakitti for sunset walks, but Lumpini feels more established and social.\n\n\n## Things to Know Before You Go\n\n• Dogs must be registered (local rules apply)\n\n• Vaccination records may be required\n\n• Keep the area clean (very important)\n\n• Best time: 6–9 AM or after 5 PM\nIt’s organized, but still relaxed which I appreciate.\n\n\n## Let’s Discuss\n\nFor those who’ve been here:\n\n• Which park do you prefer Lumpini or Benjakitti?\n\n• Is this the best pet-friendly spot in Bangkok?\n\n• Any other pet-friendly destinations in Southeast Asia worth visiting?\n\nI’m planning more pet-friendly trips, so I’d love recommendations.\n\nLet’s talk 🐾	f	f	f	110	0	0	\N	\N	active	[]	50	BMA Dog Park Bangkok: Best Pet-Friendly Spot in Lumpini &...	![Image](/images/stories/content/content-1771906345337-800147006.jpg)\n\nAs a dog owner, finding a truly pet-friendly space while traveling is not always easy....	Bangkok, BMADogPark, LumpiniPark, BenjakittiPark, PetFriendlyBangkok, DogParkAsia, UrbanTravel, ThailandTravel, DogOwnerCommunity	\N	2026-02-24 04:23:25.226+00	2026-10-15 06:46:56.635+00	0
50	8	180	Nature & Wildlife Around Tonlé Sap Lake, Cambodia – What Did You See?	nature-and-wildlife-around-tonle-sap-lake-cambodia-what-did-you-see	![Image](/images/stories/content/content-1772945577665-153907546.jpg)\n\nTonlé Sap Lake in Cambodia is often known for its floating villages, but what fascinated me the most was the natural ecosystem and wildlife surrounding the lake.\n\nAs the largest freshwater lake in Southeast Asia, Tonlé Sap changes dramatically with the seasons. During the rainy season, the lake expands and creates vast wetlands that become an important habitat for many species.\n\nWhile exploring the area, I started noticing just how rich the biodiversity here is.\n\n\n## A Unique Ecosystem in Southeast Asia\n\nTonlé Sap is part of a remarkable ecological system connected to the Mekong River. Every year, the flow of the Tonlé Sap River reverses during the monsoon, allowing water to flood the surrounding forests.\n\nThis seasonal flooding creates one of the most productive freshwater ecosystems in the world.\n\nBecause of this, the lake supports:\n\n• Hundreds of fish species\n\n• Flooded forests and wetlands\n\n• Migratory birds and waterbirds\n\n• Reptiles and other freshwater wildlife\n\nFor nature lovers, it’s a fascinating place to observe how wildlife adapts to changing water levels.\n\n\n## Birdlife Around the Lake\n\nOne of the highlights of Tonlé Sap is its birdlife.\n\nSeveral protected areas around the lake are known for bird watching, including:\n\n### Prek Toal Bird Sanctuary\n\nThis is one of the most important bird conservation areas in Southeast Asia.\n\nYou may spot species such as:\n\n• Spot-billed pelicans\n\n• Painted storks\n\n• Greater adjutant storks\n\n• Black-headed ibises\n\nDuring certain seasons, the sky around the wetlands becomes incredibly active with large colonies of birds nesting in the flooded trees.\n\n\n## Life in the Flooded Forest\n\nAnother thing that makes Tonlé Sap special is the flooded forest ecosystem.\n\nWhen the water rises, entire forests become partially submerged. These areas provide shelter and breeding grounds for fish and other aquatic life.\n\nWhile boating through the lake, I noticed how the trees stood quietly in the water, creating a landscape that feels almost surreal.\n\nIt’s peaceful, but also full of life.\n\n\n## A Different Side of Cambodia\n\nMany travelers visit Cambodia mainly for Angkor Wat, but Tonlé Sap offers something very different.\n\nInstead of temples and ancient ruins, you experience:\n\n• Wetland landscapes\n\n• Floating villages\n\n• Wildlife habitats\n\n• Traditional fishing communities\n\nIt gives a deeper look into how nature and human life coexist around the lake.\n\n\n## Let’s Discuss\n\nFor those who have visited Tonlé Sap Lake:\n\n### What wildlife did you see there?\n\n• Did you visit Prek Toal Bird Sanctuary or other conservation areas?\n\n• Were there any interesting birds, fish, or animals you noticed?\n\n• Would you recommend Tonlé Sap for nature and wildlife lovers?\n\nI’d love to hear your experiences and observations. 🌿	f	f	f	97	1	0	2026-03-10 09:33:34.051+00	22	active	[]	23	Nature & Wildlife Around Tonlé Sap Lake, Cambodia – What ...	![Image](/images/stories/content/content-1772945577665-153907546.jpg)\n\nTonlé Sap Lake in Cambodia is often known for its floating villages, but what fascinat...	TonleSapLake, CambodiaNature, CambodiaWildlife, PrekToalBirdSanctuary, SouthestAsiaWetlands, BirdwatchingCambodia, WildlifeTravel, CambodiaEcosystem, NatureExperience, CambodiaTravelDiscussion	\N	2026-03-08 04:54:58.385+00	2026-10-15 07:33:55.872+00	0
